  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced opportunities and equal treatment of vulnerable populations in the world of work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO is dedicated to improving opportunities and ensuring equal treatment for vulnerable populations in the workforce in Kenya. This Country Programme Outcome (CPO) is focused on addressing significant barriers faced by marginalized groups, including persons with disabilities, women, and young people facing high rates of discrimination and violence. Despite advances, many individuals in these groups are limited by inadequate access to social protection, employment, health, and education services.

To achieve these objectives, the ILO will enhance the capacity of stakeholders to promote gender equality, non-discrimination, and inclusion in the workplace. A key component of this effort includes developing policies and strategies aimed at eliminating stigma and discrimination related to disability and creating guidelines for inclusive employment practices. Furthermore, the ILO will facilitate capacity-building programs on the rights of persons with disabilities for both government and non-government actors.

Another critical area of intervention is addressing violence and harassment in the workplace, particularly sexual and gender-based violence (SGBV). The ILO will support the Kenyan government in its process to ratify the Violence and Harassment Convention, 2019 (No. 190), thereby establishing a framework for a safer and more inclusive work environment. This will include the creation of strategies to combat gender-based violence, enhancing the enforcement of protective measures, and facilitating awareness-raising campaigns among employers and workers.

Recognizing the pervasive issue of HIV, particularly among adolescents and young people, the ILO will work on initiatives to address the &quot;Triple Threat&quot; of new HIV infections, teenage pregnancies, and SGBV. By partnering with relevant ministries and organizations, the ILO aims to implement comprehensive awareness campaigns and enhance access to sexual and reproductive health education and services.

Benefici</narrative>
  </description>
